// lgsink.nv — the load-bearing interface: where a record goes, and what
// putting it there costs.
//
// Every body here is a `todo()`; the signatures are the design.
//
// `LgSink[e]` IS THE PACKAGE.  Everything else here is a record, a
// predicate or a string; this is the one declaration that crosses a
// layer, and the effect parameter is why it can.  SPEC § 5.6 gives a
// trait an effect parameter and a bound function may bind it, so
// `emit_if<S: LgSink[e]>(…) [e]` is written ONCE and charged exactly
// what the caller's own sink costs: `[io]` over a terminal, `[fs]` over
// a rotating file, `[mutate]` over a ring buffer, and NOTHING at all
// over the null sink or a buffer a test drains.
//
// WITHOUT THE PARAMETER, EVERY CALLER PAYS THE UNION.  The standard
// library's `Logger` shows the cost exactly: `LogSink` is an enum, so
// `Logger.info` is charged the union over its variants — `[io]` — "even
// when the installed sink is `SinkNull`", as its own page says.  A
// program that logs into a buffer and asserts on the bytes is charged
// for a console it never touches, and a caller with no `[io]` to give
// cannot log at all.  A trait with an effect parameter is the shape
// that removes that, and it is the reason this package exists beside
// `std.log` rather than wrapping it.
//
// THE SINK TAKES THE RECORD, NOT THE LINE.  The obvious surface is
// `emit(self, line: Str)`, and it is wrong in two places.  A ring buffer
// on a device would have to format on the target, which is the entire
// cost the deferred-logging story exists to avoid — the device sends an
// interned index and raw bytes precisely so that no formatter is linked
// into the firmware.  And a sink that fans out to a terminal and a
// collector would have to be handed the same record twice, pre-rendered
// two different ways, by a caller that has no reason to know there are
// two.  So the sink holds its own `LgFormat` and renders when and if it
// writes.
//
// AND THE TRAIT IS HERE RATHER THAN IN logging-nv FOR THE ONE REASON
// THIS PACKAGE EXISTS.  A `core` package may depend only on `core`
// packages, so while this declaration sat in a `host` package the trait
// designed to let a library log at `[]` was out of reach of exactly the
// libraries it was designed for.  Every impl that PERFORMS something is
// still over there — `LgStdSink` at `[io]`, `LgFileSink` at `[fs]`,
// `LgRingSink` at `[mutate]` — because each of those rows is outside a
// `core` budget by one label.  What is here is the declaration, the
// generic emits that bind `[e]`, and the one impl that costs nothing.
//
// THREE METHODS, AND `accepts` IS THE THIRD FOR A REASON.  A sink knows
// things a filter does not: a file that failed to open, a collector that
// is off, a level floor configured on the sink itself.  Asking it before
// building the record is what lets a caller skip the work, and it
// carries `[e]` like the others because SPEC § 5.6 requires every member
// of a parameterised trait to use the parameter — which is honest here
// anyway, since answering may mean looking at a descriptor.

use lgrecord

// Why a record did not reach its destination.
//
// A SINK FAULT IS NOT A PROGRAM ERROR, and the split matters at the
// call site: a logging call that answered `Result` and was `?`-ed would
// make a failed log line abort the request it was describing, which is
// never what anybody wants.  `emit` answers a `Result` so a caller CAN
// look, and every shipped sink's own recovery — `std.log`'s "a failed
// write is swallowed" — is a decision the caller makes by ignoring it,
// visibly, rather than one this package makes for everybody.
pub enum LgSinkFault
    // The transport said no.  The `IoError` is kept rather than
    // flattened to text, because a caller retrying on `IoInterrupted`
    // and giving up on `IoBrokenPipe` is the ordinary shape and a
    // string cannot be branched on.
    LgSinkIo(cause: IoError)
    // The sink was closed before this record arrived.
    LgSinkClosed
    // A bounded sink dropped records to take this one.  `dropped` is the
    // count since the last successful `flush`, so a caller can emit one
    // line saying how much it lost instead of losing it silently — which
    // is what every ring buffer that returns `Ok` does.
    LgSinkOverran(dropped: Int)
    // The sink refused this particular record: a line longer than a
    // datagram, a field the destination cannot carry.  `why` is for a
    // person and `key` names the field when one is to blame.
    LgSinkRefused(why: Str, key: Str)

// The standard `Error` implementation, so a sink failure can be
// reported by anything that reports errors.
impl Error for LgSinkFault
    // One line: which sink failed how, and what was lost.
    //
    // An overrun names the count, because "some log lines were dropped"
    // is not actionable and "412 log lines were dropped" is.
    fn message(self) -> Str
        todo("logging-core-nv.lgsink.LgSinkFault.message")

// Where a record goes.
//
// The trait binds one effect parameter, `e`, and every member uses it
// (SPEC § 5.6 requires both).  An impl supplies the set its own
// implementation actually costs: `impl LgSink[io] for LgStdSink`,
// `impl LgSink[fs] for LgFileSink` and
// `impl LgSink[mutate] for LgRingSink` are all logging-nv's, because
// each of those rows is wider than this package's budget; `impl
// LgSink[] for LgNullSink` is here, and so is any impl a `core`
// consumer writes for itself.
pub trait LgSink[e]
    // Put this record where this sink puts records.
    //
    // A sink renders it itself, through its own `LgFormat`.
    fn emit(self, r: LgRecord) -> Result<Unit, LgSinkFault> [e]
    // Push anything the sink is holding.
    //
    // A no-op for an unbuffered sink; the call that matters on the way
    // out of a program, and the call a panic handler makes before it
    // halts.
    fn flush(self) -> Result<Unit, LgSinkFault> [e]
    // Would a record at this level be written?
    //
    // Asked before the record is built, so a caller can skip the work.
    // A sink that cannot answer cheaply answers `true`.
    fn accepts(self, l: LgLevel) -> Bool [e]

// A sink that keeps nothing.
//
// `impl LgSink[]`, which is the point: a program built against the trait
// can be run with no effects at all, and a `core`-shaped test of a
// caller's logging can assert that the caller's own row did not widen.
//
// The floor is a field rather than the sink being unconditional,
// because `accepts` is a member of the trait and a sink that always
// answered `true` gives a test nothing to exercise the skip path with.
// `null_sink()` sets it to `LgTrace`, which accepts everything.
pub struct LgNullSink
    min: LgLevel

// The null sink performs nothing, which is what `[]` in the impl head
// says and what the effect checker holds it to.
impl LgSink[] for LgNullSink
    // Discard the record.
    fn emit(self, r: LgRecord) -> Result<Unit, LgSinkFault> []
        todo("logging-core-nv.lgsink.LgNullSink.emit")

    // Nothing is held, so nothing is pushed.
    fn flush(self) -> Result<Unit, LgSinkFault> []
        todo("logging-core-nv.lgsink.LgNullSink.flush")

    // Everything is accepted and nothing is kept, which is a different
    // answer from `false`: a caller measuring its own logging overhead
    // wants the record built.
    fn accepts(self, l: LgLevel) -> Bool []
        todo("logging-core-nv.lgsink.LgNullSink.accepts")

// The sink that keeps nothing.
//
// ```novo
// use lgsink
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let s = lgsink.null_sink()
//     println("${s.accepts(LgInfo)}")
// ```
pub fn null_sink() -> LgNullSink []
    todo("logging-core-nv.lgsink.null_sink")

// The sink that keeps nothing and refuses below this level.
//
// What a test of a caller's own gating uses: the caller must not build
// records this sink would refuse, and asserting that needs a sink that
// refuses.
//
// ```novo
// use lgsink
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let s = lgsink.null_sink_at(LgWarn)
//     println("${s.accepts(LgInfo)}")
// ```
pub fn null_sink_at(min: LgLevel) -> LgNullSink []
    todo("logging-core-nv.lgsink.null_sink_at")

// Emit this record if the filter and the sink both admit it, answering
// whether it was written.
//
// THE ROW IS `[e]`: this call costs exactly what `S`'s own `LgSink` impl
// costs, and that impl is declared where the sink is.  It is the whole
// argument for the trait, written as one function.
//
// The filter is checked first and the sink second, because the filter
// is arithmetic on two integers and the sink may look at a descriptor.
//
// ```novo
// use lgfilter
// use lgrecord
// use lgsink
//
// fn note<S: lgsink.LgSink[e]>(to: S, f: LgFilter) -> Result<Bool, LgSinkFault> [e]
//     lgsink.emit_if(to, f, lgrecord.record(LgInfo, "app", "started"))
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("a log call charged what the sink costs")
// ```
pub fn emit_if<S: LgSink[e]>(to: S, f: LgFilter, r: LgRecord) -> Result<Bool, LgSinkFault> [e]
    todo("logging-core-nv.lgsink.emit_if")

// Emit every record the filter admits, answering how many were written.
//
// Stops at the first fault and reports it, rather than writing the rest
// into a sink that has already said it cannot: a half-drained batch
// whose failure was swallowed is how a rotation bug becomes a missing
// hour rather than an error.
//
// ```novo
// use lgfilter
// use lgsink
//
// fn drain<S: lgsink.LgSink[e]>(to: S, f: LgFilter,
//                               rs: [LgRecord]) -> Result<Int, LgSinkFault> [e]
//     lgsink.emit_all(to, f, rs)
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("a batch charged what the sink costs")
// ```
pub fn emit_all<S: LgSink[e]>(to: S, f: LgFilter, rs: [LgRecord]) -> Result<Int, LgSinkFault> [e]
    todo("logging-core-nv.lgsink.emit_all")

// Emit and then flush, for the caller that has one batch and no more.
//
// Published because "flush at the end" is the step everybody forgets
// and the symptom — the last few seconds of a crashed process missing
// from the file — is the hardest one to notice.
//
// ```novo
// use lgfilter
// use lgsink
//
// fn finish<S: lgsink.LgSink[e]>(to: S, f: LgFilter,
//                                rs: [LgRecord]) -> Result<Int, LgSinkFault> [e]
//     lgsink.emit_and_flush(to, f, rs)
//
// fn main() [io]
//     println("the last batch, pushed")
// ```
pub fn emit_and_flush<S: LgSink[e]>(to: S, f: LgFilter,
                                    rs: [LgRecord]) -> Result<Int, LgSinkFault> [e]
    todo("logging-core-nv.lgsink.emit_and_flush")

// What a fan-out did.
//
// Counts and an optional fault rather than a `Result`, because the
// interesting outcome is PARTIAL — one sink took the batch and the
// other did not — and a `Result` has exactly two shapes to put that in,
// both of which lose a number the caller needs.
//
// THE GENERIC FAN-OUT IS ABSENT, and the reason is a language rule
// rather than a preference: a function binds exactly ONE effect
// parameter (E3005), so `fan_out<A: LgSink[e], B: LgSink[f]>` cannot be
// written and a fan-out over two arbitrary sinks has no signature.
// What exists instead is `lglog.emit_to_console_and_file`, where both
// sink types are known and the row is the concrete `[io, fs]` that pair
// costs.  A caller pairing two other sinks calls `emit_all` twice and
// keeps its own counts; the traversal is the only thing lost.
pub struct LgFanout
    first_written: Int
    second_written: Int
    // The first fault either sink reported.
    fault: ?LgSinkFault
    // `0` when there was no fault, `1` for the first sink, `2` for the
    // second.
    faulted_sink: Int
